 ## Background
-Regularization in volume rendering originated from the need to stabilize the optimization of neural radiance fields (NeRFs) and explicit voxel grids, which are prone to overfitting and artifacts due to the ill-posed nature of the inverse problem. Early works introduced Total Variation (TV) loss to enforce smoothness in voxel grids [S6, unofficial]. The concept of distortion loss was formalized to handle unbounded scenes and background collapse [S2, unofficial]. Perceptual losses were adapted from image synthesis to improve the visual quality of rendered views [S1, unofficial]. More recent developments include uncertainty-aware losses [S4] and priors on opacity distributions [S1, unofficial] to handle transparency noise. The family has evolved to include specialized losses for Gaussian Splatting [S5, unofficial], tensorial fields [S6, unofficial], and joint optimization of geometry and lighting [S1, unofficial].
+Regularization losses in volume rendering originated from the need to stabilize the optimization of neural radiance fields (NeRFs) and voxel-based representations, which are inherently ill-posed due to the ambiguity between density and color. Early works like Neural Volumes [S1, unofficial] introduced Total Variation (TV) and Beta Distribution priors to suppress floaters and enforce binary opacity. Subsequent developments, such as Mip-NeRF [S3], introduced distortion losses to handle anti-aliasing and background collapse. The family has expanded to include uncertainty-aware losses [S4], perceptual losses [S1, unofficial], and specialized geometric constraints like Chamfer Distance [S1, unofficial] and normal alignment [S1, unofficial]. Modern implementations often combine multiple regularizers (e.g., L1+SSIM [S2, unofficial], Joint Optimization [S1, unofficial]) to balance photometric fidelity with structural integrity.
 
